/****************************************************************************************
AMI AGE AT HOSPITALIZATION BY AUTOIMMUNE / CHRONIC INFLAMMATORY DISEASE
Nationwide Inpatient Sample / National Inpatient Sample (NIS)

Planned data structure, coding strategy, survey setup, models, and figures
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Data structure: one observation per NIS discharge/hospitalization. NIS represents
   hospitalizations, not unique longitudinal patients.
2. Cohort: adult hospitalizations (AGE >=18) with acute myocardial infarction (AMI) in
   the principal diagnosis field. Exclude missing age, missing sex, missing race in the
   primary complete-case cohort, and any discharge with documented old/prior MI code
   I25.2 in any diagnosis field. This exclusion reduces documented prior-MI records but
   cannot prove the AMI is a patient's first lifetime MI because NIS has no longitudinal
   patient identifiers.
3. Disease coding: AMI uses principal ICD-10-CM diagnosis codes. Autoimmune/chronic
   inflammatory diseases use all available secondary diagnosis fields and are explicitly
   non-mutually exclusive. Code lists below are clinically defensible placeholders that
   must be reviewed and edited before manuscript use.
4. Survey setup: uses NIS discharge weight, stratum, hospital cluster, and year. Replace
   the placeholder globals below if your dataset uses different variable names. For pooled
   multi-year data, the script creates year-specific stratum/cluster identifiers.
5. Models: survey-weighted descriptive analyses and survey-weighted linear regression
   with AGE as the outcome. Primary disease-specific models adjust for sex, race, year,
   Z79.5, and Z79.6; secondary models adjust for sex and race only. Premature AMI models
   use survey-weighted logistic regression for age thresholds.
6. Figures: weighted age-density plots, weighted age-category distributions, and a forest
   plot of adjusted age differences.

Suggested manuscript interpretation wording
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This descriptive cross-sectional NIS analysis evaluates age at AMI hospitalization among
hospitalized adults with AMI. NIS contains discharge-level records and does not identify
unique patients over time; therefore, the analysis cannot estimate population incidence or
risk of MI and cannot confirm first-ever MI. Excluding I25.2 removes hospitalizations with
coded evidence of old/prior MI but does not establish incident lifetime MI. Adjusted age
coefficients are descriptive associations, not causal effects. Autoimmune disease groups
are diagnosis-code based, may overlap, and may be affected by coding error or
underdocumentation. Z79.5 and Z79.6 indicate documented long-term medication use only and
capture neither dose, duration, adherence, nor all immunomodulatory therapy.
****************************************************************************************/

